WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.487
A secure deserialization boundary
establishes

2
00:00:02.487 --> 00:00:04.674
a sequence of limited guarantees.

3
00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:07.059
Parsing establishes that bytes or text

4
00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:10.040
can be interpreted in the selected format.

5
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.425
Runtime validation establishes that the
resulting

6
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:15.008
value satisfies an explicit input
contract.

7
00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:19.083
TypeScript helps maintain that contract
inside the program, but its

8
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:22.859
annotations and assertions do not create
the runtime evidence.

9
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:25.641
Keeping unverified input as unknown makes
the

10
00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:28.623
missing evidence visible without supplying
it automatically.

11
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:32.299
A useful contract covers the whole value:
required and

12
00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.976
optional fields, nullability, ranges,
formats, array contents, nested

13
00:00:35.976 --> 00:00:39.951
objects, discriminators, and a deliberate
policy for unknown properties.

14
00:00:39.951 --> 00:00:42.535
If validation strips, normalizes, or
transforms

15
00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:44.820
input, use the returned value.

16
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.205
Intentional coercion can support a
documented

17
00:00:47.205 --> 00:00:50.286
interface, but conversion alone is not
validation.

18
00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:52.671
The accepted representation must preserve
the

19
00:00:52.671 --> 00:00:54.758
meaning the application expects, including

20
00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:57.540
identifiers, boolean values, and numeric
precision.

21
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.316
Structural validity remains separate from
domain meaning and permission.

22
00:01:01.316 --> 00:01:05.092
Services enforce relationships and state
rules using trusted context.

23
00:01:05.092 --> 00:01:08.272
Authorization establishes whether the
actual caller may perform

24
00:01:08.272 --> 00:01:11.254
the requested action on the actual
resource.

25
00:01:11.254 --> 00:01:13.639
Persistence controls protect critical
invariants when

26
00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.825
concurrent activity changes that state.

27
00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:19.998
Map validated input into narrow
application commands and explicit updates.

28
00:01:19.998 --> 00:01:24.073
A transfer object describes permitted
input; a persistence entity describes

29
00:01:24.073 --> 00:01:28.644
far more of the system than an external
caller should control.

30
00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:32.023
Closed discriminators select known
behavior under server control.

31
00:01:32.023 --> 00:01:34.408
They do not justify arbitrary object

32
00:01:34.408 --> 00:01:37.587
construction, dynamic loading, or
unrestricted model hydration.

33
00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:41.662
Resource limits protect the work required
before and during validation,

34
00:01:41.662 --> 00:01:45.835
because correct parsing is not the same as
affordable parsing.

35
00:01:45.835 --> 00:01:49.015
Test the entire route through every
relevant entry

36
00:01:49.015 --> 00:01:52.096
point, including rejection before
protected side effects.

37
00:01:52.096 --> 00:01:55.872
Keep these responsibilities identifiable
as schemas and services evolve.

38
00:01:55.872 --> 00:01:59.450
The safest deserialization boundary turns
external bytes into a

39
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.524
small, validated application value—not
into a broad object that the

40
00:02:03.524 --> 00:02:07.300
rest of the system is expected to trust
automatically.

